>
  <h2>What BMI Prime Is</h2>
  <p>BMI Prime is the ratio of your actual BMI to the upper bound of the WHO healthy range (25). It collapses the six BMI categories into a single dimensionless number that is easy to interpret:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>0.74</strong> — BMI 18.5 (lower bound of healthy range)</li>
    <li><strong>1.00</strong> — BMI 25 (overweight threshold)</li>
    <li><strong>1.20</strong> — BMI 30 (obesity Class I threshold)</li>
    <li><strong>1.40</strong> — BMI 35 (obesity Class II threshold)</li>
    <li><strong>1.60</strong> — BMI 40 (severe / Class III obesity threshold)</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>BMI Prime Ranges</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Prime</th><th>Category</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>&lt; 0.74</td><td>Severely underweight</td></tr>
      <tr><td>0.74–0.84</td><td>Underweight</td></tr>
      <tr><td>0.85–0.99</td><td>Healthy range</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1.00–1.19</td><td>Overweight</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1.20–1.39</td><td>Obese</td></tr>
      <tr><td>≥ 1.40</td><td>Severely obese</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Why BMI Prime Is Useful</h2>
  <p>The standard WHO categories require memorizing five cutoffs and the corresponding clinical thresholds. BMI Prime reduces this cognitive load: a value above 1.0 means above the healthy upper bound, and the magnitude tells you how far above. A BMI Prime of 1.20 is 20% above the healthy threshold — easier to communicate and reason about than "BMI 30, Class I obesity."</p>
  <p>This makes BMI Prime particularly useful for:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Self-monitoring:</strong> A target of "BMI Prime below 1.0" is clearer than "BMI below 25."</li>
    <li><strong>Cross-population comparison:</strong> The dimensionless ratio applies equally across metric and imperial users.</li>
    <li><strong>Public health messaging:</strong> "Keep your BMI Prime below 1.0" is a single rule, easier to communicate than multiple category thresholds.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where BMI Prime Falls Short</h2>
  <p>BMI Prime inherits every limitation of BMI itself. It is still a two-compartment model that cannot distinguish muscle from fat, still uses sex- and ethnicity-blind cutoffs, and still misses visceral adiposity. BMI Prime makes the existing BMI categories more interpretable but does not fix the underlying anthropometric limitations.</p>

  <h2>When to Use BMI Prime vs BMI</h2>
  <p>For clinical communication and personal monitoring, BMI Prime offers a slight readability advantage. For research and clinical documentation, the standard BMI cutoffs are still the convention because they are universally recognized and directly mapped to clinical guidelines. Either is acceptable — choose based on your audience.</p>

